              <p class="plating-sidebar__heading">In this deep dive</p>

              <nav class="plating-toc">
                {sections.map((item) => (
                  <a
                    href={`#${item.id}`}
                    class="plating-toc__link"
                    data-toc-link={item.id}
                  >
                    <span class="plating-toc__number">{item.number}</span>
                    <span class="plating-toc__text">{item.title}</span>
                  </a>
                ))}
              </nav>

              <div class="plating-sidebar__note">
                <span class="plating-sidebar__mark">MC</span>
                <div class="plating-sidebar__note-body">
                  <p>
                    Part of the manufacturing guide. Wear is a physical process,
                    not a defect — the useful question is how fast, and where.
                  </p>

                  <a
                    href="/resources/metal-craft-guide"
                    class="plating-sidebar__back"
                  >
                    <span aria-hidden="true">←</span>
                    Back to the full guide
                  </a>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </aside>

          <!-- Body -->
          <article class="plating-content">
            <!-- Mobile contents -->
            <details class="plating-mobile-toc">
              <summary>
                <span>In this deep dive</span>
                <span class="plating-mobile-toc__icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
              </summary>
              <nav aria-label="Mobile article contents">
                {sections.map((item) => (
                  <a href={`#${item.id}`}>
                    <span>{item.number}</span>
                    {item.title}
                  </a>
                ))}
              </nav>
            </details>

            <!-- ---------- THESIS ---------- -->
            <section class="plating-thesis" aria-label="The short answer">
              <span class="plating-thesis__label">The short answer</span>
              <p>
                Plating is a metal film measured in microns, sitting on a body
                measured in millimetres. It does not fade and it does not last
                forever: it thins, and it thins fastest at the points that touch
                the world first. Every durability question is really a question
                about how fast that thinning reaches the base metal.
              </p>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- 01 ---------- -->
            <section id="film" class="plating-section" data-article-section="film">
              <header class="plating-section__header">
                <span class="plating-section__index">01</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="plating-section__eyebrow">Structure</p>
                  <h2>A film measured in microns</h2>
                </div>
              </header>

              <p>
                The body of a pin or a coin is millimetres thick. The finish on
                top of it is microns — a fraction of a hair. That ratio is the
                whole subject: the part will outlive its finish by a wide margin,
                and the finish is what the buyer sees.
              </p>

              <p>
                The stack is built in order. Nickel goes on first, because it
                bonds to brass and to zinc alloy, adds a hard layer under the
                colour, and blocks diffusion between the base metal and the
                surface. The decorative layer goes on top of that: gold, silver,
                black nickel, rose gold. On some finishes a clear coat follows,
                to take the handling instead of the metal.
              </p>

              <figure class="plating-figure">
                <div class="plating-figure__frame">
                  <svg
                    viewBox="0 0 660 292"
                    role="img"
                    aria-labelledby="stack-title stack-desc"
                  >
                    <title id="stack-title">
                      Layers of a plated finish
                    </title>
                    <desc id="stack-desc">
                      Base metal, a nickel barrier, a decorative colour layer,
                      and an optional clear coat.
                    </desc>

                    <rect
                      x="50"
                      y="60"
                      width="300"
                      height="6"
                      fill="var(--color-gold)"
                      opacity="0.22"
                      stroke="var(--color-line)"
                      stroke-width="0.75"
                      stroke-dasharray="4 3"
                    ></rect>
                    <rect
                      x="50"
                      y="66"
                      width="300"
                      height="12"
                      fill="var(--color-gold)"
                      opacity="0.6"
                      stroke="var(--color-line)"
                      stroke-width="1"
                    ></rect>
                    <rect
                      x="50"
                      y="78"
                      width="300"
                      height="24"
                      fill="var(--color-gold)"
                      opacity="0.32"
                      stroke="var(--color-line)"
                      stroke-width="1"
                    ></rect>
                    <rect
                      x="50"
                      y="102"
                      width="300"
                      height="118"
                      fill="var(--color-paper-2)"
                      stroke="var(--color-line)"
                      stroke-width="1"
                    ></rect>

                    <line
                      x1="350"
                      y1="63"
                      x2="372"
                      y2="63"
                      stroke="var(--color-line)"
                      stroke-width="0.75"
                    ></line>
                    <line
                      x1="350"
                      y1="72"
                      x2="372"
                      y2="72"
                      stroke="var(--color-line)"
                      stroke-width="0.75"
                    ></line>
                    <line
                      x1="350"
                      y1="90"
                      x2="372"
                      y2="90"
                      stroke="var(--color-line)"
                      stroke-width="0.75"
                    ></line>
                    <line
                      x1="350"
                      y1="160"
                      x2="372"
                      y2="160"
                      stroke="var(--color-line)"
                      stroke-width="0.75"
                    ></line>

                    <text
                      x="380"
                      y="63"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="11"
                      fill="var(--color-muted)"
                    >
                      clear coat — on some finishes only
                    </text>
                    <text
                      x="380"
                      y="72"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="11"
                      fill="var(--color-ink)"
                    >
                      decorative layer — the colour
                    </text>
                    <text
                      x="380"
                      y="90"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="11"
                      fill="var(--color-ink)"
                    >
                      nickel — adhesion and barrier
                    </text>
                    <text
                      x="380"
                      y="160"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="11"
                      fill="var(--color-ink)"
                    >
                      base metal — brass or zinc alloy
                    </text>

                    <text
                      x="50"
                      y="248"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="10"
                      fill="var(--color-muted-2)"
                    >
                      Not to scale. The functional layers are measured in
                      microns; the body is measured in millimetres.
                    </text>
                    <text
                      x="50"
                      y="268"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="10"
                      fill="var(--color-muted-2)"
                    >
                      Without nickel, a colour layer over zinc alloy corrodes
                      from beneath.
                    </text>
                  </svg>
                </div>
                <figcaption>
                  The order of the layers is not decorative. Each one answers a
                  different failure.
                </figcaption>
              </figure>

              <p>
                This is why “it is only plated” is a misleading phrase. A plated
                part is a metal object wearing a thinner metal skin, and the skin
                is doing three jobs at once: colour, corrosion resistance, and a
                hard surface for handling.
              </p>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- 02 ---------- -->
            <section
              id="finishes"
              class="plating-section"
              data-article-section="finishes"
            >
              <header class="plating-section__header">
                <span class="plating-section__index">02</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="plating-section__eyebrow">Finishes</p>
                  <h2>What each finish promises</h2>
                </div>
              </header>

              <p>
                A finish is not a colour. It is a colour plus a gloss plus a
                texture, and the last two decide whether wear is visible at all.
                A bright gold and a satin gold can come from the same bath and
                age completely differently, because one reflects every scratch
                and the other scatters the light that would reveal it.
              </p>

              <div class="plating-table-wrap">
                <table class="plating-table">
                  <thead>
                    <tr>
                      <th scope="col">Finish</th>
                      <th scope="col">What it looks like</th>
                      <th scope="col">How it wears</th>
                      <th scope="col">Care</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                    {finishRows.map((row) => (
                      <tr>
                        <td>{row.name}</td>
                        <td>{row.look}</td>
                        <td>{row.wear}</td>
                        <td>{row.care}</td>
                      </tr>
                    ))}
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>

              <p>
                The practical rule: the glossier the finish, the more honest it
                is about wear. A satin surface hides scratches for a year and
                then looks uniformly tired; a mirror surface tells you about
                every contact from the first week. Neither is more durable — one
                is simply more talkative.
              </p>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- 03 ---------- -->
            <section
              id="mechanisms"
              class="plating-section"
              data-article-section="mechanisms"
            >
              <header class="plating-section__header">
                <span class="plating-section__index">03</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="plating-section__eyebrow">Failure</p>
                  <h2>Four ways a finish dies</h2>
                </div>
              </header>

              <p>
                Finishes rarely fail for one reason. They fail in a sequence, and
                the sequence usually starts with something mechanical that lets
                the chemistry in.
              </p>

              <div class="plating-mechanisms">
                {mechanisms.map((item) => (
                  <div class="plating-mechanism">
                    <span class="plating-mechanism__num">{item.num}</span>
                    <h3>{item.title}</h3>
                    <p>{item.text}</p>
                  </div>
                ))}
              </div>

              <div class="plating-sequence">
                <div class="plating-sequence__step">
                  <span class="plating-sequence__label">Intact</span>
                  <p>The finish reads even under raking light.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="plating-sequence__step">
                  <span class="plating-sequence__label">Micro-scratches</span>
                  <p>Visible only at an angle. The colour is still complete.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="plating-sequence__step">
                  <span class="plating-sequence__label">Breakthrough</span>
                  <p>The layer beneath the colour shows at a high point.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="plating-sequence__step">
                  <span class="plating-sequence__label">Corrosion</span>
                  <p>Base metal reacts; the spot darkens and spreads.</p>
                </div>
              </div>

              <p>
                Read that sequence backwards and it becomes a maintenance plan:
                everything you do to delay the second stage delays all three
                that follow.
              </p>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- 04 ---------- -->
            <section id="first" class="plating-section" data-article-section="first">
              <header class="plating-section__header">
                <span class="plating-section__index">04</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="plating-section__eyebrow">Location</p>
                  <h2>Where it goes first</h2>
                </div>
              </header>

              <p>
                Wear is not evenly distributed, and it is not random. It follows
                the geometry: the highest point touches the most things, so the
                highest point loses its finish first. On a pin, that means four
                predictable places.
              </p>

              <figure class="plating-figure">
                <div class="plating-figure__frame">
                  <svg
                    viewBox="0 0 660 280"
                    role="img"
                    aria-labelledby="wear-title wear-desc"
                  >
                    <title id="wear-title">
                      Where a pin wears first
                    </title>
                    <desc id="wear-desc">
                      Three numbered points on the face — the rim, a raised metal
                      line and the flat field — with a fourth noted for the
                      reverse.
                    </desc>

                    <circle
                      cx="170"
                      cy="150"
                      r="76"
                      fill="var(--color-paper-2)"
                      stroke="var(--color-ink)"
                      stroke-width="1"
                    ></circle>
                    <circle
                      cx="170"
                      cy="150"
                      r="50"
                      fill="var(--color-gold)"
                      opacity="0.14"
                      stroke="var(--color-line)"
                      stroke-width="1"
                    ></circle>
                    <line
                      x1="120"
                      y1="150"
                      x2="220"
                      y2="150"
                      stroke="var(--color-gold)"
                      stroke-width="2"
                    ></line>

                    <line
                      x1="248"
                      y1="132"
                      x2="320"
                      y2="100"
                      stroke="var(--color-line)"
                      stroke-width="0.75"
                    ></line>
                    <line
                      x1="222"
                      y1="150"
                      x2="320"
                      y2="150"
                      stroke="var(--color-line)"
                      stroke-width="0.75"
                    ></line>
                    <line
                      x1="190"
                      y1="178"
                      x2="320"
                      y2="200"
                      stroke="var(--color-line)"
                      stroke-width="0.75"
                    ></line>

                    <circle
                      cx="248"
                      cy="132"
                      r="9"
                      fill="var(--color-paper)"
                      stroke="var(--color-gold)"
                      stroke-width="1"
                    ></circle>
                    <text
                      x="248"
                      y="132"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="9"
                      fill="var(--color-ink)"
                      text-anchor="middle"
                      dominant-baseline="central"
                    >
                      01
                    </text>
                    <circle
                      cx="222"
                      cy="150"
                      r="9"
                      fill="var(--color-paper)"
                      stroke="var(--color-gold)"
                      stroke-width="1"
                    ></circle>
                    <text
                      x="222"
                      y="150"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="9"
                      fill="var(--color-ink)"
                      text-anchor="middle"
                      dominant-baseline="central"
                    >
                      02
                    </text>
                    <circle
                      cx="190"
                      cy="178"
                      r="9"
                      fill="var(--color-paper)"
                      stroke="var(--color-gold)"
                      stroke-width="1"
                    ></circle>
                    <text
                      x="190"
                      y="178"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="9"
                      fill="var(--color-ink)"
                      text-anchor="middle"
                      dominant-baseline="central"
                    >
                      03
                    </text>

                    <text
                      x="330"
                      y="100"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="11"
                      fill="var(--color-ink)"
                    >
                      01 · the rim — first contact
                    </text>
                    <text
                      x="330"
                      y="150"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="11"
                      fill="var(--color-ink)"
                    >
                      02 · raised metal line
                    </text>
                    <text
                      x="330"
                      y="200"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="11"
                      fill="var(--color-ink)"
                    >
                      03 · flat field — dulls evenly
                    </text>

                    <text
                      x="50"
                      y="258"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="10"
                      fill="var(--color-muted-2)"
                    >
                      A front view cannot show the fourth: the reverse and the
                      clutch, which take the most handling.
                    </text>
                  </svg>
                </div>
                <figcaption>
                  Wear is geometric, not random. The same map applies to coins,
                  keychains and magnets.
                </figcaption>
              </figure>

              <p>
                The enamel method changes the map slightly. On soft enamel the
                raised lines are the high points, so they brighten and round
                first while the colour below them stays protected. On hard
                enamel the polished face is the high point, so wear arrives as a
                broad dulling across the field and a haze of fine scratches —
                less localised, and easier to see on a dark colour than on a
                light one.
              </p>

              <p>
                The reverse deserves its own sentence. It is the face that rubs
                against clothing, and the clutch is the part that fingers work
                every time the pin is put on. Neither is visible in a product
                photograph, and both are usually the first place a plated layer
                gives up.
              </p>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- 05 ---------- -->
            <section
              id="thickness"
              class="plating-section"
              data-article-section="thickness"
            >
              <header class="plating-section__header">
                <span class="plating-section__index">05</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="plating-section__eyebrow">Distribution</p>
                  <h2>Thickness is not uniform</h2>
                </div>
              </header>

              <p>
                A plating tank does not deposit an even film. Metal ions move
                with the current, and current concentrates where the geometry
                points at the anode: edges, corners, the tips of a star. Recesses
                and shielded areas sit in a lower field and receive less. The
                result is a coating that is thickest exactly where the part is
                most exposed, and thinnest where it is hardest to reach.
              </p>

              <figure class="plating-figure">
                <div class="plating-figure__frame">
                  <svg
