                    <line
                      x1="248"
                      y1="188"
                      x2="248"
                      y2="168"
                      stroke="var(--color-gold)"
                      stroke-width="10"
                    ></line>
                    <line
                      x1="252"
                      y1="166"
                      x2="300"
                      y2="166"
                      stroke="var(--color-gold)"
                      stroke-width="8"
                    ></line>

                    <line
                      x1="112"
                      y1="178"
                      x2="322"
                      y2="96"
                      stroke="var(--color-line)"
                      stroke-width="0.75"
                    ></line>
                    <line
                      x1="180"
                      y1="186"
                      x2="322"
                      y2="150"
                      stroke="var(--color-line)"
                      stroke-width="0.75"
                    ></line>
                    <line
                      x1="276"
                      y1="166"
                      x2="322"
                      y2="206"
                      stroke="var(--color-line)"
                      stroke-width="0.75"
                    ></line>

                    <text
                      x="330"
                      y="96"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="11"
                      fill="var(--color-ink)"
                    >
                      at an edge — thicker
                    </text>
                    <text
                      x="330"
                      y="150"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="11"
                      fill="var(--color-ink)"
                    >
                      inside a recess — thinner
                    </text>
                    <text
                      x="330"
                      y="206"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="11"
                      fill="var(--color-ink)"
                    >
                      on a flat — nominal
                    </text>

                    <text
                      x="60"
                      y="250"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="10"
                      fill="var(--color-muted-2)"
                    >
                      Schematic. The film follows current density, not the
                      drawing.
                    </text>
                  </svg>
                </div>
                <figcaption>
                  The gold band is the plated layer. Its thickness changes with
                  the geometry of the part, before any wear happens.
                </figcaption>
              </figure>

              <p>
                Three consequences follow. First, “thicker plating” is not a
                specification unless it names where it was measured — a part can
                be generous at the rim and thin in the field. Second, the nickel
                layer matters more than the colour layer for corrosion: a thick
                colour over a thin barrier still fails from beneath. Third,
                thicker is not free — a heavier deposit rounds fine detail and
                shifts the colour slightly, which is a real trade on a design
                that depends on hairlines.
              </p>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- 06 ---------- -->
            <section
              id="control"
              class="plating-section"
              data-article-section="control"
            >
              <header class="plating-section__header">
                <span class="plating-section__index">06</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="plating-section__eyebrow">Our workshop</p>
                  <h2>What we control</h2>
                </div>
              </header>

              <p>
                Durability is decided by five things that happen before the part
                is packed, and none of them are visible in a finished photograph.
              </p>

              <div class="plating-controls">
                {controls.map((item) => (
                  <div class="plating-control">
                    <span class="plating-control__num">{item.num}</span>
                    <div>
                      <h3>{item.title}</h3>
                      <p>{item.text}</p>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                ))}
              </div>

              <div class="plating-note">
                <p class="plating-note__label">Salt spray: reference, not a promise</p>
                <p class="plating-note__foot">
                  Neutral salt spray to {saltSpray.standard} is the usual way to
                  compare finishes. The figures usually quoted —
                  {saltSpray.reference} — are reference points rather than a
                  guarantee. {saltSpray.note}
                </p>
              </div>

              <p>
                The point of testing is not the certificate. It is that a batch
                with a number attached can be compared with the batch before it,
                and a complaint six months later can be answered with a record
                instead of an opinion.
              </p>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- 07 ---------- -->
            <section id="care" class="plating-section" data-article-section="care">
              <header class="plating-section__header">
                <span class="plating-section__index">07</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="plating-section__eyebrow">Maintenance</p>
                  <h2>Care that works, care that doesn't</h2>
                </div>
              </header>

              <p>
                Care is not about polishing a piece back to new. It is about
                keeping the second stage of the sequence — the micro-scratches —
                from arriving early. Most of the damage done to plated parts is
                done by people trying to clean them.
              </p>

              <div class="plating-care">
                <div class="plating-care__col">
                  <p class="plating-care__label">That helps</p>
                  <ul>
                    {careDo.map((item) => (
                      <li>{item}</li>
                    ))}
                  </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="plating-care__col plating-care__col--warn">
                  <p class="plating-care__label">That damages</p>
                  <ul>
                    {careDont.map((item) => (
                      <li>{item}</li>
                    ))}
                  </ul>
                </div>
              </div>

              <p>
                One distinction worth keeping: on an antique finish, the dark
                recess is the design, and the high points are meant to brighten
                with handling. That is patina, not wear. On a bright finish, the
                same brightening is the first stage of loss. The finish tells you
                which one you are looking at.
              </p>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- 08 ---------- -->
            <section
              id="verdict"
              class="plating-section"
              data-article-section="verdict"
            >
              <header class="plating-section__header">
                <span class="plating-section__index">08</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="plating-section__eyebrow">Judgement</p>
                  <h2>Wear or defect?</h2>
                </div>
              </header>

              <p>
                This is the only question that matters when a complaint is
                raised, and it has a clean answer: wear follows use, defects do
                not. A finish that has thinned where the part is handled is doing
                what physics says it must. A finish that blisters inside a recess
                nobody touches has failed as a process.
              </p>

              <div class="plating-verdict">
                <div class="plating-verdict__col">
                  <p class="plating-verdict__label">Wear</p>
                  <ul>
                    {wearSigns.map((item) => (
                      <li>{item}</li>
                    ))}
                  </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="plating-verdict__col plating-verdict__col--defect">
                  <p class="plating-verdict__label">Defect</p>
                  <ul>
                    {defectSigns.map((item) => (
                      <li>{item}</li>
                    ))}
                  </ul>
                </div>
              </div>

              <p>
                If a claim is being made, three photographs settle most of it: a
                raking-light shot of the whole part, a macro of the affected
                edge, and a shot of the batch label. Re-plating a small part is
                rarely worth the handling; the honest answer is usually a
                remake, and the useful question is which batch it came from.
              </p>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- CLOSING ---------- -->
            <footer class="plating-conclusion">
              <div class="plating-conclusion__rule"></div>
              <p class="plating-conclusion__label">The decision</p>

              <div class="plating-rule">
                <p>
                  <strong>Expect wear at the high points.</strong> The question
                  is not whether the rim will lose its finish, but how soon — and
                  that is decided by use, care and the layer stack.
                </p>
                <p>
                  <strong>Specify the stack and the test, not the adjective.</strong>
                  “Durable plating” is a mood; nickel under colour, measured
                  thickness and a salt spray record are a specification.
                </p>
                <p>
                  <strong>Judge wear against the sample and the calendar.</strong>
                  A part that thinned after a year of daily wear is not defective;
                  one that corroded in a recess in three weeks is.
                </p>
              </div>

              <p class="plating-conclusion__text">
                If you are choosing a finish for a piece that will be worn rather
                than displayed, say so at the brief stage. The right answer for a
                display coin and the right answer for a daily-worn pin are
                different finishes, and the difference is cheaper to decide now
                than to explain later.
              </p>

              <div class="plating-conclusion__signature">
                <span>METALCRAFTS</span>
                <span>CRAFT · DETAIL · QUALITY</span>
